WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.659
In about two months, we'll all be playing Sekiro, a game

00:00:03.659 --> 00:00:07.588
that's really shaking the foundations of the soulsborn franchise.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:13.710
And the hardest thing for this community to wrap its head around has been the lack of RPG elements.

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:16.469
Sekiro has no stat level up screen.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:20.310
You're forced to use a katana and there are fewer gear choices.

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:26.250
But recently it has been revealed that Sekiro still has a surprising amount of character customization;

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:31.289
No, you can't dress up as an onion of the Deep or princess Lautrec,

00:00:31.509 --> 00:00:33.839
but you can level up your character,

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:40.590
you can diversify your play style, and you can even spec into different combos and special attacks.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:46.860
This is all done, for the first time, with something simply called experience; as you kill enemies

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:49.469
you'll rack up experience points, which

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:53.159
actually aren't lost upon death, unlike in other souls games.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:59.609
Once you have enough you head back to the hub world, and then you invest them into three talent trees;

00:00:59.739 --> 00:01:06.389
One of these trees is called Shinobi, another one is called Prosthetic and the third is called Samurai.

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:10.409
It reminds me a lot of World of Warcraft's old talent system.

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:14.280
You know, back when World of Warcraft didn't feel like a mobile game.

00:01:14.619 --> 00:01:20.758
So just like this, actually, these talent trees will contain both active abilities that you can unlock and

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:26.519
also passive effects that you can invest in. So let's take the Shinobi talent tree,

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:32.430
for example; from what I understand this would enhance things like your critical strikes, your stealth,

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:36.629
basically everything assassin related. Take wall-jumping

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:37.320
for example.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:45.299
This would be an example of a(n) active ability and an example of a passive Shinobi ability might be reducing your aggro range

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:52.259
during stealth, something like that. I'm sure you guys can think of more. Second, the Prosthetic talent tree;

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:54.100
This one's pretty self-explanatory.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:59.790
An active ability in this tree might be unlocking combo attacks for your prosthetics,

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:06.299
like how flame vent can set your sword on fire or how your shuriken arm can follow up with a katana slash.

00:02:07.119 --> 00:02:09.428
A passive effect in this tree would be

00:02:10.069 --> 00:02:15.159
increasing the amount of shurikens thrown for example, as seen here, or maybe

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:17.679
reducing the amount of spirit emblems

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:22.060
you need to activate an attack. And third, the Samurai talent tree,

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:27.099
which I think will be my favorite, is likely going to focus on general combat;

00:02:27.319 --> 00:02:29.319
so, in terms of active abilities,

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:36.580
we know already that there are special attacks that you can perform by holding down r1 and l1, like this cross slash attack

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:37.459
for example,

00:02:37.459 --> 00:02:41.738
so I bet there will be tons of other varieties to unlock in this tree. An

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:46.989
example of a passive ability in this tree might be making you deal more posture damage, or

00:02:47.239 --> 00:02:52.479
allowing your healing gourd to replenish more health. And apparently with enough experience

00:02:52.579 --> 00:02:56.079
you can actually upgrade every talent in this tree and

00:02:56.690 --> 00:03:03.190
100% complete your character, but it's unlikely that you'll be able to do this in a single playthrough. And

00:03:03.799 --> 00:03:10.389
experience isn't the only form of currency; there's also gold, another static resource that we use to trade with vendors,

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:17.709
there's even apparently crafting materials that we can use to enhance our prosthetic arm. So, this is pretty great.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:22.690
the more you explore the world the more enemies you fight and the more you enhance your character and

00:03:23.209 --> 00:03:30.129
diversify your play style. You can lament the loss of a stat system all you like really, but you kind of have to admit,

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:33.519
grinding for a new attack or a new skill,

00:03:34.069 --> 00:03:38.979
is a bit more exciting than grinding to upgrade one point of dexterity.

00:03:39.109 --> 00:03:43.899
-not that I level Dex or anything- Leveling up is done at the hub location

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:50.410
which is called The Dilapidated Temple, a place located on the Ashina Outskirts and, like in other games,

00:03:50.989 --> 00:03:54.099
NPCs will occasionally travel here for quests or for trading,

00:03:54.739 --> 00:03:57.969
but we already know of three who will be there every time;

00:03:58.669 --> 00:04:06.519
First, The Sculptor. As expected, he's the one we go to to upgrade our prosthetic arm with materials that we find throughout the world.

00:04:07.069 --> 00:04:10.809
Second, there's going to be a woman there named Emma. She's responsible for

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:14.739
upgrading the amount of charges your healing gourd can contain,

00:04:15.469 --> 00:04:21.998
upgraded with an item called spirit water drops. And third, and my favorite, The Immortal Soldier.

00:04:21.999 --> 00:04:26.349
I love this. Basically, he's a swordsman that's incapable of dying

00:04:26.349 --> 00:04:33.218
and so he serves as a sort of human training dummy who we can whale on and practice our moveset with. I

00:04:33.349 --> 00:04:39.399
really hope he whinges is about it, like Crestfallen would. I have a feeling that'll be the same sort of character. And,

00:04:40.099 --> 00:04:42.968
being able to practice combat here is

00:04:43.669 --> 00:04:48.818
way way way more important in Sekiro than it has been in other souls games

00:04:49.159 --> 00:04:55.149
because in Sekiro the hub will also be the place where we invest in skill trees and abilities that

00:04:55.639 --> 00:05:01.269
fundamentally change the way our character plays, so it makes sense to be able to test these in a safe environment.

00:05:01.849 --> 00:05:09.099
Outside of the talent tree, if you want a permanent upgrade to your health or posture, this is done through an item called Prayer Beads.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:13.029
Apparently these are commonly dropped by mini bosses in Sekiro and

00:05:13.729 --> 00:05:20.348
that's a good reason to fight these mini bosses instead of running past them, because yes running past things is

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:22.810
probably gonna be pretty

00:05:22.879 --> 00:05:27.908
effective in this game. According to Miyazaki the environments are fairly open in the beginning

00:05:27.909 --> 00:05:32.889
but he also said that they really open up around the halfway mark in the game, and

00:05:33.649 --> 00:05:39.549
open up they should because not only can we grapple over things, we can swim.

00:05:40.099 --> 00:05:43.658
That's right. No longer do we need to fear knee-deep water.

00:05:44.149 --> 00:05:45.110
Imagine

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:50.319
sneaking into a castle via a water duct or fighting a boss that swims beneath you.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:55.659
In Game Informer's two-hour session Sekiro explored the Hirata estates, and

00:05:56.120 --> 00:06:00.789
the name Hirata should be familiar to some of you who watched this video;

00:06:01.399 --> 00:06:07.689
Apparently we travel back to this place in a memory and this is accessed via the hub world which raises the question

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:13.359
by visiting this memory are we simply reliving something that has already happened, or

00:06:13.819 --> 00:06:17.588
are we capable of going back in time to change the past, and,

00:06:18.649 --> 00:06:24.429
considering we still have our ninja prosthetic in all of these scenes, I'm leaning towards the latter.

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:29.049
I think we might be able to change things here. The village is being razed to the ground

00:06:29.590 --> 00:06:32.410
by drunken bandits. I can't wait to figure out why.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:37.750
Second mini-boss of this stage is one of these drunken bandits. His name is Juzou

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:42.160
The Drunkard. In his second phase he spits poison onto his weapon

00:06:42.230 --> 00:06:48.519
which means it will probably damage you if you try to parry him, and this is a cool example of how they can subvert the

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:51.609
core gameplay in Sekiro, because everyone's playing in a similar way,

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:57.550
so everyone is going to have to find a way around this, regardless of your built. Apparently during this mini boss

00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:01.359
you can find a nearby NPC who will help you during this fight,

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:07.660
but since there's no summoning system in Sekiro, you know, there's no online, there's no Soapstones or anything,

00:07:08.390 --> 00:07:13.570
reports say that if this NPC dies during the fight it might be permanent.

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:17.829
Not everyone is undead this time, you have to remember. And

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:22.929
the concept of death permanence in Sekiro, for NPCs at least

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:28.480
could mark a pretty huge departure from all of the years and years of undead

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:32.080
characters we've had with souls games; in those games

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:38.620
it was often difficult to pin down a canon death for the character but in Sekiro, with

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:43.780
well-defined death comes more emotional stories and, speaking of emotion,

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:48.130
apparently some of these NPCs now have their own soundtracks.

00:07:48.440 --> 00:07:52.540
We've known for a while that the music is being composed by the legendary

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:57.910
Yuka Kitamura, who has done in-house music for many From games before this and

00:07:58.460 --> 00:08:04.210
she's going to have her hands full because there's more music in this game than in any other previous title.

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:06.940
Not only is there the music for boss battles,

00:08:07.070 --> 00:08:14.470
there is music during stealth, during combat, during exploration, and there are even songs created for specific

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:19.810
characters said to enhance the emotions associated with their stories, and

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:27.009
as someone who spends hours and hours finding the perfect music to suit these characters in "Prepare to Cry"

00:08:27.230 --> 00:08:29.319
this last point makes me pretty excited.

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:37.119
Think how fitting Gwyn's theme is for his character and then apply that to some of your favourite characters in these games,

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:39.280
they kind of all deserve it.

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:41.380
All of this pales in comparison to,

00:08:41.979 --> 00:08:44.249
perhaps the biggest news this week;

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:48.630
Sekiro has a pause button. I'll just let that sink in a bit.

00:08:49.029 --> 00:08:53.578
It actually makes more sense in this game since Sekiro is about killing ingeniously,

00:08:53.980 --> 00:09:01.019
you can apparently pause and get an opportunity to rifle through your equipment and your items for a solution to your problem.

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:05.789
The biggest curiosity that remains, I think, is the death penalty.

00:09:06.490 --> 00:09:13.769
Miyazaki himself recently said that it's still taking shape, and it's very unique, and it's more personal than ever before, and

00:09:13.959 --> 00:09:19.349
he also said that it's not just going to be the classic corpse run to retrieve your souls.

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:26.099
So what could it be? I want you guys to help me out with this one. I reckon together we'll probably stumble across it.

00:09:26.470 --> 00:09:33.540
So here are my current ideas; First, since Miyazaki says that the death penalty for dying is more personal,

00:09:34.149 --> 00:09:39.149
then I think perhaps it's our prince who is keeping us alive with his blood.

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:46.079
Our prince, after all, in the last trailer said "Wolf, along with my blood, you must live."

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:50.849
I wouldn't be surprised if with every death we weaken our prince. Or,

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:56.130
perhaps with every death we develop a more difficult world state, you know

00:09:56.130 --> 00:09:58.469
just like world tendency in Demon Souls,

00:09:58.870 --> 00:10:06.269
perhaps enemies grow stronger every time we die. Since we pray to these statues which are called demon Buddha statues,

00:10:06.730 --> 00:10:14.459
what if Sekiro himself becomes more demon-like with every death? Imagine a sort of deal with the devil every time you die.

00:10:15.130 --> 00:10:21.569
And finally, Bloodborne had an insight meter. What if our perception of the world changes negatively with every death?

00:10:22.209 --> 00:10:23.440
These are some ideas.

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:30.149
Can you think of others? Let me know in the comments below and, if you're watching this video as it goes live right now,

00:10:30.639 --> 00:10:36.359
chances are I'm streaming right now. So, come over to Twitch and say hi. I'll see you all next time.

